     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>That just means they are &gt; 9999, so there is no point printing the ugly mess.  When they are even a few hundred, the t distribution is effectively the normal distribution.</div><div><br></div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422100601000&amp;usg=AOvVaw12aaYvQn7CkNU6JVHclXYL\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div></div><div><br></div><div> </div></div>"
